                    <occupation>teacher of Italian</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">Godwin buys a copy of <title>Baretti and Graglia</title> - an edited 
    form of <title>Baretti's Dictionary</title>, on 6.05.1790 and on 31.05.1790 notes 'M. Curioni, 1st lesson' - suggesting that he is learning Italian with Curioni.
    <title>The Times</title> advertises Curioni's lectures on 'Italian Authors in Prose and Verse' and his availability for private teaching.
    It also refers to his work on the Italian, French and English languages, his membership of the Academy of the 
    Arcadi at Rome, and his being Professor of Languages at the Lyceum in Paris.
    

  </note>
